{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"All four research agents are done. Here's the synthesis, then the intent questions — because the research genuinely changes the picture.\n\n## What I found\n\n**conversation-ledger is much further along than turnbridge's docs assume.** It's working v0.1 code, not a design: a `cledger` CLI storing immutable events as canonical JSONL in git notes (`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`), anchored to HEAD, with content-hash event IDs for idempotent re-capture. Critically, it *already ships* the capture side turnbridge planned to own: `cledger install claude-code|codex` sets up hooks, `cledger capture --transcript` parses both native JSONL formats, with per-session cursors and dedup. Turnbridge's stated division (\"Turnbridge owns CLI-specific adapters; Ledger owns the durable record\") doesn't match reality — the ledger owns the adapters today. Gaps: no importable library API (bin-only), no redaction, no purge, no squash-merge re-anchoring.\n\n**intent-recall** is a design-stage downstream consumer that reads the ledger to answer \"what behavior was intended?\" It treats turnbridge as *one producer among several* and mainly constrains the event schema (immutability, actor identity, supersession links) — which the ledger already satisfies.\n\n**sticky-note** (BandaruDheeraj) is a different animal: git-backed *team handoff* memory — threads, files-touched, failed approaches, narrative summaries — injected into teammates' next sessions via hooks/MCP, across Claude Code, Copilot CLI, and Codex. It's about summarized shared context between *people*, not lossless transcript portability.\n\n**git-ai** is git-ai-project/git-ai (~2.3k★): line-level AI code *attribution* stored in git notes — same storage trick as your ledger, but no transcripts, no resume. The popular one you forgot is almost certainly **SpecStory** (wraps agent CLIs, auto-saves sessions to `.specstory/history/*.md`). More interesting: ther